# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_THREAD_CREATE */
int64_t thread_create(uint32_t process, const char *name, uint64_t name_len,
                      uint64_t options, uint32_t *out);
/* CROI_SYS_THREAD_START */
int64_t thread_start(uint32_t thread, uint64_t entry, uint64_t stack,
                     uint64_t arg1, uint64_t arg2);
/* CROI_SYS_THREAD_EXIT */
int64_t thread_exit(int64_t code);
/* CROI_SYS_THREAD_READ_STATE */
int64_t thread_read_state(uint32_t thread, uint32_t kind, void *buffer,
                          uint64_t size);
/* CROI_SYS_THREAD_WRITE_STATE */
int64_t thread_write_state(uint32_t thread, uint32_t kind, const void *buffer,
                           uint64_t size);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A thread is one flow of control in a process, with its own registers and stack.

## thread_create

`thread_create` makes a thread in <process>, which needs MANAGE_THREAD, and writes its handle to <out>.
<name> and <name_len> are as for process_create(2); <options> must be 0.
The handle has the basic rights and READ, WRITE, GET_PROPERTY, SET_PROPERTY, DESTROY, SIGNAL and MANAGE_THREAD.
The thread does not run until it is started.
A process that has ended is `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`.

## thread_start

`thread_start` starts <thread>, which needs MANAGE_THREAD, at user address <entry> with stack pointer <stack>.
The process must already be running: its first thread is started by process_start(2).
The thread starts with <arg1> in its first argument register, <arg2> in the second and the vDSO's base address in the third.
A thread already started, or a process not running, is `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`; an <entry> that is not a user address is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
A thread that starts while its process is suspended starts suspended.

## thread_exit

`thread_exit` ends the calling thread.
It needs no handle and does not return.
<code> is not kept.
If it is the process's last running thread, the process ends.
The thread's handle asserts `CROI_SIGNAL_TASK_TERMINATED`.

## thread_read_state, thread_write_state

These read and write the registers of a thread that is stopped: suspended (task_suspend(2)) or waiting in an exception (exception(2)).
Any other thread is `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`.
Reading needs READ on the thread's handle, writing needs WRITE.
<kind> picks the state:

: `CROI_THREAD_STATE_GENERAL_REGS`
  A `croi_thread_state_general_regs_t`, the registers in the order of the architecture's layout in `<croi/syscall.h>`.
  Reading needs <size> at least its size (`CROI_ERR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL`), writing exactly its size (`CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`).
  A write changes only the flags user code may set (the arithmetic flags on amd64, NZCV on arm64).
  On amd64 `fs_base` and `gs_base` must be user addresses.
: `CROI_THREAD_STATE_DEBUG_REGS`
  A `croi_thread_state_debug_regs_t`: the debug registers on amd64 (DR0 to DR3 must be 0 or user addresses; DR6 and DR7 keep only their user bits) and the breakpoint and watchpoint registers on arm64, whose counts and the last watchpoint hit are read only.
  On rv64 there is nothing and a write is ignored.
: `CROI_THREAD_STATE_SINGLE_STEP`
  A `uint32_t`: 1 if the thread executes one instruction when it resumes, 0 if not.
  Not available on rv64.

Writing the debug registers or single-step needs the kernel booted with `croi.debug_syscalls=on` (or a test boot), else `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.
Any other <kind> is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.

